blazer a ui framework for building  front-end web applications with html css  and not javascript but c-sharp by  leveraging the power of webassembly it's  able to eliminate javascript from your  front-end stack it was created at  microsoft in 2018 by steve sanderson the  developer famous for creating knockout  js years earlier just like react and  other modern ui libraries you build  components called razer components that  encapsulate parts of the user interface  what makes blazer special is its ability  to blur the lines between client and  server thanks to wasm you can run c  sharp code with your favorite.net  libraries in the browser or  alternatively run your client side code  on the server and stream changes back  into the dom via signal ir a tool built  on top of websockets for real-time  communication in addition blazer  components are capable of running on any  platform with dotnet maui you can reuse  your code natively on ios android or  desktop now if you're not convinced  c-sharp can get the job done by itself  it can easily interop with javascript on  both the client and server to get  started install visual studio with  asp.net capabilities then generate a new  blazer webassembly project the root of  your application contains a router in  the app.razer file notice how it's  structured with a variety of built-in  components like router and layout view  that don't need to be explicitly  imported like a javascript app now  create a new page by adding a razer  component to the pages directory at the  top of that file add the page directive  to specify the url pattern we can then  add a title using a built-in razer  component or simply add some plain html  here but now it's time to add some  reactive state to this application which  can be accomplished with the code  directive which allows us to write logic  with the industrial strength reliability  of c sharp define a property like count  then use it in a template with the at  symbol the value is reactive so any time  it changes the template will re-render  we can add a method to the code to  increment the count then bind it to a  button as the on click event handler now  to make the component look pretty add a  matching css file and all the styles  here will be scoped to that component  now when it comes to sharing logic and  state between components blazer has  dependency injection built in which  allows you to write a class in one place  then inject it into any component using  the inject directive in addition when  you add a component to the shared  directory it can be used instantly  throughout the entire application and  not only runs on the web but virtually  any native platform now use net run to  compile the web assembly and view your  web app in the browser this has been  blazer in 100 seconds hit the like  button if you want to see more short  videos like this thanks for watching and  i will see you in the next one
 

